WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello  halloween may be long gone but we have  yet to cover the scariest thing in zelda  history the bane of 90s nintendo players  conduit of childhood trauma slayer of  parents phone bills the infamous water  temple  [Music]  the impact of this single video game  level cannot be understated just look up  the ost for this place and you'll find  countless comments and memes about the  frustration and anguish these people  felt when they first set foot into these  unholy rooms and corridors and to those  people i say yeah me too i think we all  got our ass handed to us in one way or  another during this stage of the game  but is this because it really was that  hard well to some degree yes yes it was  but not necessarily because of the  dungeon itself i think the majority of  our problems and struggles can be  chalked up to the following a many of us  were relatively young when we first  played ocarina of time b 3d gaming was  still a bit of a novel concept for many  people around this time and navigating  in three dimensions wasn't as intuitive  as it is now nowadays people make fun of  the fact that na'vi has to explain to  link how to open a door or push a block  but this kind of stuff really was  necessary for a lot of people coming  from the 16-bit era to get acclimated to  this new way of playing video games and  see ocarina of time may be a timeless  classic to many but it definitely had  some great abs built into its game  design that made things unnecessarily  frustrating and obtuse now don't get me  wrong what nintendo managed to achieve  back then is nothing short of legendary  and it's no surprise why the game is  still regarded as one of the best video  games ever made they took a formula that  was born in the 2d era and successfully  translated it to a new format overcoming  many hurdles 3d action adventure games  were challenged with all the while  inventing brand new gameplay mechanics  that are still used today albeit refined  they did it before with mario and they  did it again here but it was still the  early days of 3d gaming and obviously  they didn't get everything right on the  first try the water temple is one of  those places where some of these  limitations are emphasized like the  camera as much as the camera did its job  in wide open areas it definitely  struggled in small confined spaces  especially tight corridors and of course  when discussing the water temple it's  impossible to get around its most  prominent flaw the one issue that has  been hammered into the ground by this  point that being the way the iron boots  are utilized we all know the deal when  inside a body of water the normal boots  make you float while the iron boots make  you sink to the bottom every time you  need to swap between the two you have to  pause the game navigate to the equipment  section select the boots you want to  equip and exit the pause menu every damn  time even when playing at peak  efficiency meaning you know exactly  where to go skipping optional rooms and  utilizing diving whenever possible you  will still be hitting that pause button  around 30 times throughout the whole  experience and yes i counted and that's  excluding all the times you may need to  pause for different reasons like  changing weapons or checking the map and  like i said that's if you know what  you're doing on your first playthrough  that number is likely going to be twice  probably three times that amount or even  more this alone makes the water tempo a  drag to get through and i honestly have  no idea how it made it through play  testing back in the day i mean someone  had to have taken notice of this right  it's also no surprise then that nintendo  rectified this mistake for the 3ds  remake which made the boots function as  an item you can map to the face buttons  instead but even with this improvement i  still feel like it isn't enough to  unlock the dungeon's full potential yes  descending and ascending is now quicker  and easier but underwater traversal is  still slow and tedious your mobility is  still heavily crippled and having to  rely on the hookshot alone and not being  able to use your sword is frustrating  and it's a shame because there's a lot  to like about the water temple in fact i  would argue that underneath all the  clunkiness lies one of the smartest well  thought out and most original dungeons  in the series it's also one of only a  few 3d zelda dungeons that can compete  with the complexity of some of the  earlier 2d ones it's like a miniature  metroidvania level there's a lot of  directions to choose from and many  places you aren't able to visit until  later forcing you to keep track of where  you have or haven't been the water  temple is all about roadblocks and  demands that the player understands how  its many rooms and corridors are  connected and how the different water  levels affect those connections as the  cherry on top it also has one of the  coolest and most memorable mini bosses  in zelda dungeon history so with all  that said let's take a closer look at  the water temple how it's designed what  made it difficult and how the layout and  progression works  despite the fact that the water temple  is generally considered to be one of the  hardest dungeons to navigate the flip  side is that it's also the dungeon you  are probably the least likely to die in  the enemy variety is pretty low and they  aren't exactly among the most difficult  ones they don't do a lot of damage and  aside from some very cheap scenarios  where you're helpless to fight back  getting hit by them is mostly a matter  of impatience at best they can be  annoying especially when you're on the  water but there's enough refills to get  you through no problem you might die on  dark link i mean dark larry on your  first try when you haven't figured out  how to deal with them yet but since the  actual final boss has an absolute  pushover chances are you won't see a  game over screen very often especially  if you came prepared with medicine or  fairies in spite of its confusing layout  the water temple only has four floors  even less than the very first dungeon  inside the great deku tree but it's the  many branching paths and the way you  access one floor from another that makes  it disorienting compared to all the  other dungeons in many of these you  don't really take note of the different  floors because they don't affect each  other all that much at the very most you  might cross the same room from a  different elevation or drop down to a  chamber below but that's about the  extent of it in the water temple the  different floors are everything it  dictates where you can go how you can  get there and when you can get there  depending on the water level dropping or  raising the water will grant access to  certain rooms while at the same time  barring off others a good example being  these floating platforms even if you  manage to make your way to the third or  second floor without manipulating the  water first you still won't be able to  access some of the rooms here because  the platforms are no longer there to  support link alternatively some rooms  can only be accessed by using a bomb or  the bow and arrow which cannot be used  while submerged you enter the dungeon  from the top floor and straight into the  central chamber and this is where the  intimidation factor of the water temple  immediately becomes apparent most of  ocarina of times dungeons have a central  chamber all of which have multiple exits  and entrance ways for instance the  forest temple has seven the spirit  temple has six seven if you count the  hidden door behind the statue's face  which you won't find out about until the  very end including the entrance the  water temple has twice that amount 14  possible ways of either entering or  exiting the main chamber and there's  also an additional space inside the  central pillar which houses the only way  in and out of the basement so yeah it's  no wonder why people were a bit  overwhelmed from my own experience the  water temple has four distinct phases  there's the where do i go face the key  phase the boss key phase and finishing  up with the boss fight the first phase  as the name elegantly suggests is the  initial moment after first entering the  dungeon when you have no idea where to  even start so many doors so many  hallways to choose from especially on  the first playthrough it can be pretty  overwhelming but when you take a step  back take a breath and look at the  bigger picture you'll quickly find that  with a bit of methodical exploration the  dungeon does give you some direction  right off the bat only one out of four  doorways on the top floor is accessible  and this one path doesn't even lead to  anything except a block you can pull and  doing so will obstruct the hallway  behind it it is advised that you do this  by the way as it will save some time  later on but anyway yeah a dead end  right away so you head back to the  central room with the third floor  eliminated the only option that remains  is to go down and go down we shall the  second floor is almost as useless as the  third floor at this stage but if you  want to be a rebel you can get the  compass early since one of the four  doorways is accessible whether submerged  or not but this path only leads into one  room containing the compass and nothing  else and never has to be revisited again  after so all that leaves is the first  floor which is where most of the early  progression takes place like i said as  intimidating as the dungeon can feel  when first entering it does actually  guide you in a certain direction and  automatically funnels you towards the  bottom drawing you down floor by floor  by process of elimination once at the  bottom you again only have one way to go  the other four pathways either lead to  an immediate dead end or are completely  blocked altogether this one requires a  bomb which doesn't work on the water  this one can only be accessed with a  long shot and the other two cannot be  reached not without the floating  platforms i mentioned earlier which are  still floating on the third floor so all  that leaves us is this room which is  also where we meet up with princess ruto  this room is also quite genius because  it acts as your first test to see if you  understand the central mechanic of the  dungeon and the consequences the  different water levels have it's sort of  a miniature version of the central  chamber it too has three floors with  each floor granting or preventing access  to a room depending on the water level  ruto will explain that you'll be able to  manipulate the water to three different  levels after which she guides you up to  the third floor but as you drift upwards  you'll be able to catch a glimpse of the  second floor which shows a cracked wall  you're currently unable to blow up  because again bombs don't work on the  water at least not until twilight  princess what i find respectable about  the water temple is that it lets you  make these kinds of connections on your  own there's enough time between  observing that cracked wall on the  second floor and gaining access to the  second water level that you could  potentially forget about it and get  stumped later on there's no reminders no  companions popping up and being like oh  look at that cracked wall i wonder what  that's all about in fact for a companion  that's so often criticized and memed  about because of her intrusive nature  she sure is quiet during this dungeon  and thank god  it respects your intelligence and trust  that you'll make a mental note out of it  and still be able to recall it by the  time you raise the water to the second  floor so as you reach the third floor  you'll find the first panel with which  to manipulate the water level in this  case draining it all the way down to the  first floor there's also an additional  room leading to the map and i think it's  no coincidence that they give you the  map and compass fairly early on as it's  definitely one of those dungeons where  they are almost crucial on the first  playthrough speaking of the map i do  have to go on a brief tangent here  because as nice as it is to have and it  definitely is i do have one issue with  the way it works and this has everything  to do with the way it displays certain  rooms there's quite a lot of cases where  the same room will show up on multiple  floors now for some rooms like the  central chamber this does make sense as  it spans across different floors it's an  important aspect about it but for a room  like this for example it really doesn't  notice how as you submerge and resurface  even slightly the map will constantly  swap between the basement level and the  first floor even though this room  doesn't really have anything to do with  the basement nor is it accessible from  there the ceiling is also not  particularly high but apparently the map  still finds it necessary to include the  room on two different floors this  creates a lot of duplicate rooms on the  map and makes it more confusing than it  needs to be i think it would have been  better if they just kept the rooms  visible only on the floors where they  are relevant even if you're technically  dipping slightly below or above set  floor just because this room has a  bottomless pit that stretches all the  way to the bottom of the dungeon does it  really need to be shown on every floor  anyway from here we entered the key  phase because as you've noticed we  haven't collected one single key up to  this point with a total of 6 the water  temple has one of the highest key counts  out of all ocarina's dungeons rivaled  only by the fire temple which has eight  but it's the way you collect them that  makes the water temple stand out and the  majority are collected and spent in the  first half of the dungeon before  obtaining the key item the entire goal  of this phase is to somehow find your  way back to the third floor raise the  water back to the third level as well so  the platform is there while also being  in possession of two keys when you get  there the doors to the second and third  water panel both require a key as well  so that's a total of four keys you need  to collect before you can even reach the  dungeon item these four keys are located  here two on the first floor one in the  basement and one on the second floor  behind that cracked wall i mentioned  earlier the tricky part is that if you  miss even one of these four keys you'll  never be able to get to the dungeon item  of course that goes without saying but  if you're unlucky you'll also have to  start the entire cycle all over again  because of the way the dungeon works  it's not always as easy as just diving  back down and getting the key because  remember certain doors are inaccessible  depending on the water level see you  can't access the second panel while the  water is at level three you won't be  able to get there and even if you could  the ocarina doesn't work on the water  for obvious reasons  likewise you cannot get to the first  panel from level 2 as the water isn't  high enough to reach it you always have  to go from 3 to 1 then 2 back to 3 so on  and so forth so say for instance you get  to the 3rd floor only to realize that  you forgot the key behind that cracked  wall which embarrassingly enough  actually did happen to me on a recent  live stream wait there's not a key door  there right oh there is i forgot a key  this means having to dive all the way  back down float up to the first panel  lowering the water to the first floor  entering the central pillar to get to  the second panel raise the water to the  second floor dive back down to the first  floor float back up this room to the  second floor blow up the wall and obtain  the key and then of course you need to  go back up to raise the water to the  third floor and you're back on track if  you know exactly what you're doing you  only have to change the water level a  total of six times throughout the whole  experience but on a first playthrough  you won't know how many keys you'll need  to move forward it's not like you can  guess that there's another locked door  right behind this one so you'll likely  be repeating this cycle at least one or  two times before getting to the long  shot not to mention that not all keys  are easy to find there's of course the  key sneakily hidden in the basement  speaking of which this is also where i  want to make a quick comparison between  the n64 original and the 3ds version  because this is one example where i feel  like they definitely overcorrected the  entrance to the basement is hidden  underneath this block and becomes  accessible when you raise the water to  the second floor and the block floats  upwards and yeah it's a pretty sneaky  location to hide a key but it's not like  it's impossible to find out when the  cutscene plays on the n64 the camera  follows the block as it rises upwards  and if you're a little bit observant you  can actually see the entrance if you  would check the map it also clearly  shows that there's another room right  below this one it doesn't spell it out  for you but it's also not as convoluted  as some people claim it to be but i  guess many people disagreed because for  the 3ds they totally removed the  challenge not only did they change the  angle of the camera during the cutscene  but it also literally zooms in on the  entrance as if to say over here dummy it  completely destroys any incentive from  the player to find out on their own they  might as well have added a giant arrow  and a neon sign there as well i  definitely had a lot of trouble with the  water tempo as a kid but i still vividly  remember how clever i felt when i found  this out on my own on the n64 it was  subtle but not unfair here it cannot  even be classified as a discovery the  game does it for you the same could be  said about these impossible to miss  glowing guidelines which weren't there  on the n64 they're meant to guide you to  the different water panels and although  i find them unnecessary they don't  bother me as much because it's not like  the water panels were hard to find on  the n64 either anyway once you have all  the keys you need it's a straight shot  to dark link and subsequently the long  shot you send your darker half back to  the abyss claim the long shot and  congratulations you're about 60 of the  way there from here you'll automatically  be guided back to a room on the first  floor you've already visited before you  also collect the second to last key on  the way there which is impossible to  miss so now you're essentially back in  familiar territory but with no guidance  where to go next accessing the boss  chamber only requires two things the  long shot and the boss key this next  phase can be a bit of a hassle if you  just mindlessly start running around and  exhausting every option but if you use  the map you would see that there's  really only three remaining pathways you  haven't visited one leading to the boss  and two leading to a chest one  containing the final small key and the  other the boss key it's actually pretty  straightforward at the very most you  might go after the wrong chest first as  the map doesn't differentiate between a  normal chest and the boss chest if you  happen to go after the boss key first  you'll quickly realize that the very  very last door of this section is locked  and this is really where i started to  realize my incompetence and impatience  back when i first played ocarina of time  because i'm pretty sure this is exactly  what happened to me i would stumble upon  this final locked door and be like oh my  god head back into the dungeon  mindlessly run around like an idiot  until i finally found the last key on  the whim i barely looked at the map if  at all i don't know maybe i was just a  dumb kid but the fact that so many  people have such bad memories of this  place gives me a shimmer of hope that  maybe a lot of us were dumb and  impatient back when we were kids and  it's not just me so yeah you get the  last key it's behind that block we  pulled at the beginning you get the boss  key go back to the third floor enter the  boss room and defeat the boss the end  and that's the water temple for you was  it hard well yes and no compared to most  other dungeons in ocarina it's  definitely a significant step up in  difficulty the others don't require you  to backtrack to this extent sure there's  some keys that are hidden quite well and  you might have to go back and get it but  it doesn't happen very often during the  key phase and boss key phase especially  you will be backtracking quite a lot on  a first playthrough and there's a lot of  roadblocks to keep in the back of your  mind for later this is why i stated that  the water temple is a bit like a  miniature metroidvania stage and might  also explain why some people had great  difficulty while others barely had any  trouble with it at all i think it boils  down to experience if you grew up  playing games like super metroid then  getting lost checking maps and  backtracking are natural for you and the  water temple was probably small potatoes  but if you're someone like me who mostly  stuck to mario and pokemon prior to  picking up ocarina of time you probably  lack the experience to explore  methodically heck ocarina of time was my  very first zelda so i didn't even have  the experience from the older games at  that point so yes for some of us the  water temple really was that hard but it  definitely doesn't mean that the dungeon  is bad gameplay wise it is and now numa  even apologized for it around the time  of ocarina of time 3d's release but he  also apologized for the overall design  of the dungeon itself and that's where  i'm like no don't apologize for the  dungeon design it's good the way all  these mechanics work together the water  levels the platforms the maze-like  interior it's brilliant it's one of the  most clever designs in the series and  there's a reason why more and more  critics have come forward to express  their appreciation for it it's a dungeon  that demands patience if you run around  like a headless chicken without thinking  about where you're gonna go and why  you're gonna have a bad time but if you  take your time and explore methodically  utilize the map and learn to understand  its mechanics and layout it's one of the  most satisfying dungeons to conquer the  only thing holding it back and unlocking  its true potential is the clunky  underwater controls 3ds improvements are  not i'm convinced that if ocry enough  time had similar underwater controls as  twilight princess being able to swim up  and down freely instead of only relying  on the iron boots using your sword  underwater etc this dungeon wouldn't  nearly be as divisive and may have even  been regarded as one of the best in the  series by almost everyone because  mechanically it really is anyway that  wraps up the video i want to thank  everyone for their viewership support  and all that good jazz a shout out to my  amazing discord community and a big  thanks to my patreons and channel  members you are all amazing as always  and that is all for now this is dawn  signing off and have a good one  [Music]  foreign

